WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.760
It's clearly a pretty obvious thing
to stick a bit of filling

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.480
between two slices of something
along the lines of bread

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.920
and eat it with your hands,

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.800
but the British sandwich industry
is worth £8 billion a year.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.080
Across Western Europe and America
sandwiches are booming.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.840
Some made in-home
but most bought on the run.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.200
It's been done
for thousands of years,

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.280
whether it's with flatbread or
yeasted loaves, cheese or meat,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.680
especially by those eating
away from a table.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.920
For instance, working on the fields
or as a casual snack.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.480
It wasn't named though

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.040
or even recognised
as a thing which needed a name

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.480
until the 18th Century.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.080
The story goes that John Montagu,
the 4th Earl of Sandwich,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:38.800
a town on the
south coast of England,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.080
was at the gambling table one night,
decided not to stop for supper,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.680
and ordered a simple snack of beef
between two slices of bread.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.920
The real story
is a bit less clear cut.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.200
Montagu wasn't a big spender,
he was broke most of the time,

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.160
and his idea
didn’t come out of nowhere.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.560
He'd almost certainly seen
pitta breads used with a filling

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.680
on his travels
in the Eastern Mediterranean.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.000
Whatever the truth, the concept
was certainly named after him,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.200
and the word
was in fashionable use by the 1770s.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.280
Early sandwiches
stayed close to the original idea,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.680
they were designed for quick eating

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.840
initially by men
doing business at coffee houses

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.040
or playing cards,

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.960
and then for more mixed tea parties,
 ball buffets and light suppers.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.320
Early 19th Century sandwiches
were intended to be elegant,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.000
often served on doilies
with the crusts cut off.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.480
But the name was also used
for the age-old worker's meal -

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.400
thick bread, butter
and a universal filling of ham.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.440
And by the middle
of the Victorian period

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.840
sandwich sellers clustered
around every factory gate.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.280
One writer called them
mouth-distorters,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.680
and pleaded for sandwich makers
to make them delicate and delicious.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.160
His suggested fillings included:

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.520
Bone marrow and nasturtium,
potted woodcock,

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.320
sliced sweetbreads, sea kale
and oranges soaked in brandy.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.440
A more humble alternative was
provided

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.720
by best-selling author
Isabella Beeton.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.880
She suggested a toast sandwich -

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.200
literally a slice of cold toast
between two slices of bread,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.920
well-seasoned with salt and pepper.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:02.360
It was, apparently, particularly
well-suited for invalids.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.680
In the 20th Century

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.080
the British sandwich of
two slices of bread and a filling

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.600
was joined by other alternatives
from overseas.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.880
The Scandinavian open sandwich
was praised by British food writer

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.920
Marguerite Patten in the 1960s.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.040
While filled pittas,
croissants and wraps

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.040
have also crept in to join
the basic bread and butter version.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.880
Toasted sandwiches
came out of the States in the 1920s

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.800
when the Toastie-Maker was patented.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.320
The US had become sandwich-central,

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.800
even briefly having a national
Sandwich Day on November the 3rd -

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.680
the original
Earl of Sandwich's birthday.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.120
But it wasn't until 1980

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.560
that the pre-packed sandwich
made its first appearance

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.800
in Marks and Spencer
and initially only in five stores.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.280
They were an instant hit

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.400
and now the working lunch
is almost synonymous

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.160
with the prawn cocktail sandwich -

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.080
still M&amp;S's biggest seller.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.120
The science behind constructing
a non-soggy sandwich

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.120
which can last all day
is very big business indeed.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.040
Of course,
the predictable fillings still vie

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.440
with slightly more
avant-garde attempts.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.880
Lasange-wich anyone?

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.320
And many sandwich aficionados
swear blind

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.600
that the biggest improvement
to any bought sandwich

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.720
is a layer of ready-salted crisps.

